// Part of the MIND project (Machine Intelligence Native Design).
//
// WHAT THIS DEMONSTRATES, AND WHY IT IS NOT ANOTHER OPTION MODEL.  The other
// examples under `examples/quant` all price optionality: Black-Scholes, the
// higher-order Greeks, a binomial lattice, an implied-vol root-find.  Every one
// of them is an EQUITY DERIVATIVES witness.  A fixed-income risk desk does not
// read those; it reads a price, a yield, a duration and a convexity, and it is
// asked to reproduce all four on demand.
//
// This module is that witness.  Nothing here is optional, stochastic, or
// model-dependent: a bond's price given a yield is an arithmetic identity, and
// so is its duration.  That is exactly what makes it valuable as a determinism
// artifact.  When a regulator asks "reproduce the 31 March duration of this
// position", there is no model assumption to hide behind — either the number
// comes back bit-for-bit or the firm's reproduction claim is false.  The
// quantities below are the ones actually asked for.
//
// THE CLAIM, STATED EXACTLY.  This module does NOT claim to match Bloomberg, a
// vendor analytics library, or any particular market convention in the last
// bits.  It cannot, and it is not trying to: market conventions differ on day
// count, settlement, and accrued interest, and two correct systems following
// different conventions produce different numbers for good reasons.  The claim
// is narrower and harder:
//     the same source, compiled for two different ISAs, produces byte-identical
//     output, with a stated accuracy bound against a 50-digit oracle.
// Accuracy and reproducibility are orthogonal properties.  Determinism is what
// makes a reproduction claim meaningful, not a substitute for a convention.
//
// SCOPE, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-READ.  This is a CLEAN-PRICE, WHOLE-PERIOD
// model: cashflows land on exact period boundaries i/freq for i = 1..n, and
// there is no settlement date, no accrued interest, and no day-count fraction.
// A real desk needs all three.  They are deliberately absent because each one
// is a CONVENTION (30/360 vs ACT/ACT vs ACT/360), and baking one convention
// into a numeric contract is how two systems that agree on the mathematics
// disagree on the number.  Adding them is a wrapper around this core, not a
// change to it.
//
// NO `pow`, AND WHY THAT IS A SAFETY PROPERTY, NOT A LIMITATION.  Every
// discount factor here is (1 + y/f)^(-i) for an INTEGER i, and is built by a
// fixed left-to-right repeated multiply rather than by a transcendental power.
// Two reasons, both load-bearing:
//   1. An integer power by repeated multiplication is a finite sequence of
//      exact-order IEEE multiplies.  A `pow` built on exp(log(x)*y) rounds
//      twice through two transcendentals and is strictly worse here.
//   2. A MIND function whose name matches a C99 libm symbol (exp, log, pow,
//      sin, cos, erf, erfc) can be constant-folded to the HOST libm by LLVM
//      when the argument is a compile-time constant, silently skipping the MIND
//      body and destroying cross-substrate identity.  This is a documented
//      defect in this repo.  Every kernel below is therefore named `dm_*` or
//      `det_*`, never the bare libm name.  Keep that discipline.
//
// SELF-CONTAINED, AND WHY.  The detmath kernels are duplicated below instead of
// imported.  mindc 0.10.2 cannot carry an f64 across a module boundary (roadmap
// 17.1): an imported `fn f(x: f64) -> f64` types as `(i64) -> i64` at the call
// site and miscompiles silently.  This mirrors `examples/quant/black_scholes`
// and `examples/detmath_kat`.  The block between the KERNEL BEGIN and KERNEL
// END markers is copied BYTE-IDENTICALLY from
// `examples/quant/black_scholes/main.mind`, so the two examples cannot silently
// diverge in their kernels.  Only the subset actually used here is vendored:
// erf, erfc, norm_cdf and norm_pdf are absent because a bond has no normal
// distribution in it, and vendoring an unused kernel is dead code that nothing
// gates.
// deferred: replace the duplicated kernel block with `import std.detmath;` once
// roadmap 17.1 lands — upgrade path: delete the block between the KERNEL BEGIN
// and KERNEL END markers and call the std surface directly.
//
// NO `[test]` FUNCTIONS, AND WHY.  The mindc 0.10.2 `[test]` interpreter
// mis-evaluates an f64 comparison in a function body (it takes the then-branch
// unconditionally).  Every guarded function here would return through its
// leading `if x != x` guard and every interpreted assertion would pass
// vacuously — a false green.  The native backend evaluates f64 branches
// correctly, so the gate is build-and-run: `mindc run`, exit code 0 means every
// check passed.  The count of failures is the exit code, so a partial failure
// is never rounded to success.
//
// REFERENCES (original sources only):
//   Macaulay, F. R. (1938). "Some Theoretical Problems Suggested by the
//     Movements of Interest Rates, Bond Yields and Stock Prices in the United
//     States since 1856." NBER.  (Defines the duration measure.)
//   Hicks, J. R. (1939). "Value and Capital." Oxford.  (Independently derives
//     the same measure as the elasticity of price to the discount factor —
//     the modified-duration form.)
//   Redington, F. M. (1952). "Review of the Principles of Life-Office
//     Valuations." Journal of the Institute of Actuaries 78(3), 286-340.
//     (Second-order matching; the reason convexity is a reported number.)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// =========================== KERNEL BEGIN ==================================
// Duplicated verbatim from std/detmath.mind (roadmap 17.1 blocks importing).
// Do not "improve" these: every polynomial is a fixed left-fold Horner
// recurrence in source order.  Reassociating one changes the last bit of every
// price downstream and breaks the cross-substrate hash.

fn absf(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - x;
    }
    return x;
}

// +inf by byte-identical IEEE overflow; never a synthesised NaN.
fn inf() -> f64 {
    let mut v: f64 = 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < 350 {
        v = v * 10.0;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// 0x7FF8000000000000 — the MIND-canonical quiet NaN, pinned so an
// out-of-domain result is byte-identical (x86 sets the sign bit, ARM does not).
fn canonical_qnan() -> f64 {
    return __mind_bits_to_f64(9221120237041090560);
}

fn rnd_to_i64(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x >= 0.0 {
        return (x + 0.5) as i64;
    }
    return 0 - ((0.5 - x) as i64);
}

fn det_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2_hi: f64 = 0.6931467056274414;
    let ln2_lo: f64 = 0.00000047493250390941725;
    let inv_ln2: f64 = 1.4426950408889634;
    let k: i64 = rnd_to_i64(x * inv_ln2);
    let kf: f64 = k as f64;
    let r: f64 = (x - kf * ln2_hi) - kf * ln2_lo;
    let mut t: f64 = 0.0000000000007647163731819816;
    t = 0.00000000016059043836821613 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000000208767569878681 + r * t;
    t = 0.00000002505210838544172 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000002755731922398589 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000027557319223985893 + r * t;
    t = 0.0000248015873015873 + r * t;
    t = 0.0001984126984126984 + r * t;
    t = 0.001388888888888889 + r * t;
    t = 0.008333333333333333 + r * t;
    t = 0.041666666666666664 + r * t;
    t = 0.16666666666666666 + r * t;
    t = 0.5 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    t = 1.0 + r * t;
    let mut y: f64 = t * 1.0;
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    if k >= 0 {
        while j < k {
            y = y * 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    } else {
        while j < (0 - k) {
            y = y / 2.0;
            j = j + 1;
        }
    }
    return y;
}

fn dm_exp(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x >= 709.7827128933841 {
        return inf();
    }
    if x <= 0.0 - 745.1332191019412 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    return det_exp(x);
}

fn det_log_core(x: f64) -> f64 {
    let ln2: f64 = 0.6931471805599453;
    let mut k: i64 = 0;
    // f64-param-init: seed a loop-carried f64 binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut m: f64 = x * 1.0;
    while m >= 1.4142135623730951 {
        m = m / 2.0;
        k = k + 1;
    }
    while m < 0.7071067811865476 {
        m = m * 2.0;
        k = k - 1;
    }
    let s: f64 = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0);
    let s2: f64 = s * s;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0;
    let mut n: i64 = 29;
    while n >= 1 {
        acc = 1.0 / (n as f64) + s2 * acc;
        n = n - 2;
    }
    return (k as f64) * ln2 + 2.0 * s * acc;
}

fn dm_log(x: f64) -> f64 {
    if x != x {
        return x;
    }
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0;
    }
    if x == 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - inf();
    }
    if x >= inf() {
        return inf();
    }
    return det_log_core(x);
}

// ============================ KERNEL END ===================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DISCOUNTING PRIMITIVES.
//
// A bond's discount factor is (1 + y/f)^(-i) for an INTEGER period index i.
// `df_periodic` builds it as a fixed left-to-right repeated multiply of the
// single-period factor.  The loop runs exactly i times, ascending, and the
// accumulation order is part of the contract: multiplying the same factors in a
// different order is algebraically identical and numerically different, and
// would change the last bit of every price, duration and convexity below.
//
// The single-period factor is formed ONCE as `1.0 / (1.0 + y / f)` and then
// reused, rather than recomputing the division inside the loop.  That is not an
// optimisation — it is a determinism requirement: one division rounded once,
// then multiplied, is a fixed rounding sequence, whereas a division per step
// would be a different one, and the two disagree in the last bits.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Single-period discount factor v = 1/(1 + y/f).
fn df_single(ytm: f64, freq: f64) -> f64 {
    return 1.0 / (1.0 + ytm / freq);
}

// v^i by repeated multiply, ascending, fixed order.  i is an i64 period COUNT,
// never a real exponent: there is no `pow` on this path and there must not be.
fn df_pow(v: f64, i: i64) -> f64 {
    // f64-param-init: seed the loop-carried binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut acc: f64 = 1.0 * 1.0;
    let mut j: i64 = 0;
    while j < i {
        acc = acc * v;
        j = j + 1;
    }
    return acc;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ADMISSIBILITY.
//
// Every public function returns the canonical qNaN on a degenerate input rather
// than a plausible-looking number.  A silent numeric fallback is how a pricing
// bug reaches production wearing a valid-looking price — and in fixed income
// the valid-looking price is the one that gets booked.
//
// The yield condition is `1 + y/f > 0`, not `y > 0`.  Negative yields are real
// and must price: a -50bp two-year Bund is an ordinary instrument.  What is
// inadmissible is a yield at or below -f, where the single-period factor is
// infinite or negative and the "price" is arithmetic noise.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
fn bond_valid(face: f64, coupon_rate: f64, ytm: f64, freq: i64, n_periods: i64) -> i64 {
    if face != face {
        return 0;
    }
    if coupon_rate != coupon_rate {
        return 0;
    }
    if ytm != ytm {
        return 0;
    }
    if freq <= 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if n_periods <= 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if face <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if coupon_rate < 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let f: f64 = freq as f64;
    if 1.0 + ytm / f <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. BOND PRICE.
//
//   P = sum_{i=1..n} (face * c / f) * v^i  +  face * v^n,     v = 1/(1 + y/f)
//
// SUMMATION ORDER IS PINNED, AND THIS IS THE LOAD-BEARING COMMENT OF THE FILE.
// The coupon leg is accumulated strictly ascending in i, one `acc = acc + term`
// per period, and the principal repayment is added LAST as a separate
// statement after the loop.  This order is part of the numeric contract:
//
//   * Summing descending (longest cashflow first) is algebraically identical
//     and numerically different — it is in fact slightly MORE accurate, because
//     it adds the small terms together before they meet the large ones.  It is
//     still forbidden here, because "more accurate" is not the property being
//     defended.  Reproducibility is.
//   * A compiler permitted to reassociate this sum (a vectorised tree
//     reduction, four partial accumulators summed at the end) would produce a
//     different last bit on a machine with a different vector width.  That is
//     precisely the cross-substrate divergence this repository exists to
//     prevent: the same source would price the same bond differently on x86
//     `avx2` and ARM `neon`.
//   * The principal is added after the loop rather than folded into the final
//     coupon term for the same reason: it is a distinct rounding step, and
//     which step it is must not depend on how the loop was compiled.
//
// So: do not reassociate, do not reorder, do not fuse.  The scalar strict-FP
// path is the contract, not an implementation detail.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
pub fn bond_price(face: f64, coupon_rate: f64, ytm: f64, freq: i64, n_periods: i64) -> f64 {
    if bond_valid(face, coupon_rate, ytm, freq, n_periods) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let f: f64 = freq as f64;
    let v: f64 = df_single(ytm, f);
    let cpn: f64 = face * coupon_rate / f;
    // f64-param-init: `0.0 * 1.0` seeds the accumulator as f64 under 0.10.2.
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0 * 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 1;
    while i <= n_periods {
        acc = acc + cpn * df_pow(v, i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    // Principal repayment, added LAST and as its own rounding step.
    acc = acc + face * df_pow(v, n_periods);
    return acc;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. YIELD TO MATURITY.
//
// The inverse of `bond_price` in y.  There is no closed form for n > 4, so this
// is a root-find, and a root-find is where reproducibility is usually lost.
//
// WHY THE ITERATION COUNT IS FIXED, AND WHY A TOLERANCE LOOP IS FORBIDDEN.
// The obvious implementation is
//     while |f(y)| > tol { y = y - f(y)/f'(y); }
// and it is NOT reproducible, for a reason that has nothing to do with the
// tolerance being loose.  The number of times that loop runs is a function of
// the ROUNDING of f(y).  Two substrates that agree on the mathematics but
// differ by one ulp in a single intermediate — because one fused a
// multiply-add and the other did not, or because a library exp rounded
// differently — will cross the tolerance on DIFFERENT iterations.  One does 7
// steps, the other does 8.  The extra step changes the answer in the last bits,
// and now the two substrates disagree.
//
// The divergence is worse than it first appears: the trip count is a CONTROL
// FLOW property, so it is not bounded by the size of the numeric perturbation
// that caused it.  A one-ulp difference does not produce a one-ulp difference
// in the answer; it produces a whole extra Newton step.
//
// So the trip count here is a compile-time literal.  It is chosen once, for the
// widest admissible bracket, and paid unconditionally on every call — including
// on the calls that converged after four steps.  The cost is a few dozen
// multiplies.  The purchase is that the control flow is identical on every
// substrate for every input, which is the only way the answer can be.
//
// THE THREE CONSTANTS ARE THE ENTIRE CONTROL-FLOW CONTRACT.  None is derived
// from an input; none is compared against a tolerance.
//   YTM_LO    = -0.9   — below this, `1 + y/f` is non-positive for f = 1 and the
//                        price is not defined.  Deeply negative yields inside
//                        this bound still price, by design.
//   YTM_HI    =  2.0   — 200% annualised.  A bond quoted at a yield above this
//                        is in default, not in a pricing model.
//   YTM_ITERS =  80    — FIXED.  80 guaranteed-or-better halvings of a width-2.9
//                        interval reach 2.4e-24, far below one ulp of any yield
//                        in range; the accepted Newton steps only make it
//                        converge sooner.  The count does not vary with the
//                        inputs.
//
// The structure mirrors `examples/quant/implied_vol`: a bracketed Newton with a
// bisection fallback, where the bracket is updated BEFORE the Newton candidate
// is vetted, so an overshooting Newton step is rejected rather than trusted.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn ytm_lo() -> f64 {
    return 0.0 - 0.9;
}

fn ytm_hi() -> f64 {
    return 2.0;
}

fn ytm_iters() -> i64 {
    return 80;
}

// dP/dy, analytic.  Used as the Newton derivative.
//
//   dP/dy = -(1/f) * sum_{i=1..n} i * cf_i * v^(i+1)
//
// Analytic, NOT a finite difference: a bumped-and-revalued derivative would
// inherit two prices' worth of rounding AND a step-size choice, and the step
// size would become one more thing the iteration's trajectory depends on —
// i.e. one more way two substrates could take different paths.
fn bond_dprice_dy(face: f64, coupon_rate: f64, ytm: f64, freq: i64, n_periods: i64) -> f64 {
    if bond_valid(face, coupon_rate, ytm, freq, n_periods) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let f: f64 = freq as f64;
    let v: f64 = df_single(ytm, f);
    let cpn: f64 = face * coupon_rate / f;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0 * 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 1;
    while i <= n_periods {
        acc = acc + (i as f64) * cpn * df_pow(v, i + 1);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    acc = acc + (n_periods as f64) * face * df_pow(v, n_periods + 1);
    return 0.0 - acc / f;
}

// Admissibility for the inverse.  Returns 1 only when a yield in
// [YTM_LO, YTM_HI] can actually reproduce `price`.
//
// The bounds are computed with the SAME pricer the solver inverts, so a price
// admitted here is one the pricer can actually produce.  Bisection on a bracket
// that contains no root converges confidently to an endpoint — that is the
// single most dangerous failure mode of a root-find, because the answer looks
// converged and carries no evidence that it is wrong.  Reject instead.
//
// Note the direction: price is strictly DECREASING in yield, so the price at
// YTM_LO is the HIGH price and the price at YTM_HI is the LOW one.  Getting
// this comparison backwards would reject every valid input, which the KAT
// catches, and admit every invalid one, which only the domain checks catch.
fn ytm_admissible(price: f64, face: f64, coupon_rate: f64, freq: i64, n_periods: i64) -> i64 {
    if price != price {
        return 0;
    }
    if price <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if bond_valid(face, coupon_rate, ytm_lo(), freq, n_periods) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if bond_valid(face, coupon_rate, ytm_hi(), freq, n_periods) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let p_hi: f64 = bond_price(face, coupon_rate, ytm_lo(), freq, n_periods);
    let p_lo: f64 = bond_price(face, coupon_rate, ytm_hi(), freq, n_periods);
    if p_hi != p_hi {
        return 0;
    }
    if p_lo != p_lo {
        return 0;
    }
    if price > p_hi {
        return 0;
    }
    if price < p_lo {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// The fixed-count bracketed Newton/bisection hybrid.
//
// INVARIANT, maintained at every step: the root lies in [lo, hi].  It holds at
// entry because `ytm_admissible` proved the price is between p(hi) and p(lo),
// and the price is strictly monotone decreasing in yield for a non-negative
// coupon.  It is preserved by the sign test, which discards only the half that
// cannot contain the root.
//
// The loop body is branch-balanced: every iteration evaluates the price once
// and the derivative once, regardless of which branch it takes.  The only
// input-dependent thing is WHICH of the two updates is applied, never HOW MANY
// times the body runs.
pub fn bond_ytm(price: f64, face: f64, coupon_rate: f64, freq: i64, n_periods: i64) -> f64 {
    if ytm_admissible(price, face, coupon_rate, freq, n_periods) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    // f64-param-init: seed each loop-carried f64 binding via the exact identity
    // `x * 1.0` so mindc 0.10.2 does not infer it as i64.
    let mut lo: f64 = ytm_lo() * 1.0;
    let mut hi: f64 = ytm_hi() * 1.0;
    let mut y: f64 = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < ytm_iters() {
        let fy: f64 = bond_price(face, coupon_rate, y, freq, n_periods) - price;
        // Bracket update FIRST.  Price DECREASES in yield, so an overpriced
        // model value (fy > 0) means the yield is too LOW: move the lower
        // bound up.  This is the sign that is easiest to get backwards, and
        // getting it backwards still "converges" — to the wrong endpoint.
        if fy > 0.0 {
            lo = y;
        } else {
            hi = y;
        }
        let d: f64 = bond_dprice_dy(face, coupon_rate, y, freq, n_periods);
        // Newton candidate, defaulting to the bisection midpoint.  Guarded on
        // d < 0.0 (the derivative is strictly negative for an ordinary bond)
        // because a zero or positive derivative would send the step out of the
        // bracket on exactly the iteration where the bracket is the only thing
        // keeping the iteration honest.
        let mut cand: f64 = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
        if d < 0.0 {
            let nn: f64 = y - fy / d;
            // STRICTLY inside.  A candidate equal to an endpoint is rejected:
            // accepting it would let the iterate stall on a bracket edge for
            // the remaining fixed iterations without the interval shrinking.
            if nn > lo {
                if nn < hi {
                    cand = nn;
                }
            }
        }
        y = cand;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return y;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. DURATION AND CONVEXITY — ANALYTIC, NOT BUMPED.
//
//   Macaulay D  = ( sum_i t_i * cf_i * v^i ) / P,          t_i = i / f
//   Modified D  = D / (1 + y/f)
//   Convexity C = ( sum_i t_i * (t_i + 1/f) * cf_i * v^(i+2) ) / P
//
// These are the closed-form sums, evaluated in the SAME pinned ascending order
// as the price.  They are not finite differences of the pricer, for the reason
// stated throughout this repository: a bumped-and-revalued sensitivity inherits
// two prices' worth of rounding plus a step-size choice, and the step size then
// becomes part of the numeric contract without being written down anywhere.
//
// The convexity convention here is the standard one: the second derivative of
// price with respect to yield, divided by price, with the periodic compounding
// carried correctly through the (t + 1/f) factor.  It is quoted per 1.00 of
// yield, NOT per 100bp.  A desk wanting "per basis point" divides at the call
// site; scaling it here would bake a presentation convention into a numeric
// contract.
//
// The finite difference appears exactly ONCE in this file: in the KAT, as a
// CHECK on modified duration.  That is the appropriate use of a bump — to
// falsify an analytic formula, never to implement one.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pub fn macaulay_duration(face: f64, coupon_rate: f64, ytm: f64, freq: i64, n_periods: i64) -> f64 {
    if bond_valid(face, coupon_rate, ytm, freq, n_periods) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let f: f64 = freq as f64;
    let v: f64 = df_single(ytm, f);
    let cpn: f64 = face * coupon_rate / f;
    let p: f64 = bond_price(face, coupon_rate, ytm, freq, n_periods);
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0 * 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 1;
    while i <= n_periods {
        let t: f64 = (i as f64) / f;
        acc = acc + t * cpn * df_pow(v, i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    let tn: f64 = (n_periods as f64) / f;
    acc = acc + tn * face * df_pow(v, n_periods);
    return acc / p;
}

pub fn modified_duration(face: f64, coupon_rate: f64, ytm: f64, freq: i64, n_periods: i64) -> f64 {
    if bond_valid(face, coupon_rate, ytm, freq, n_periods) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let f: f64 = freq as f64;
    let d: f64 = macaulay_duration(face, coupon_rate, ytm, freq, n_periods);
    return d / (1.0 + ytm / f);
}

pub fn convexity(face: f64, coupon_rate: f64, ytm: f64, freq: i64, n_periods: i64) -> f64 {
    if bond_valid(face, coupon_rate, ytm, freq, n_periods) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let f: f64 = freq as f64;
    let v: f64 = df_single(ytm, f);
    let cpn: f64 = face * coupon_rate / f;
    let p: f64 = bond_price(face, coupon_rate, ytm, freq, n_periods);
    let inv_f: f64 = 1.0 / f;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0 * 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 1;
    while i <= n_periods {
        let t: f64 = (i as f64) / f;
        // Exponent i+2, NOT i+1.  Differentiating v^i = (1+y/f)^(-i) twice with
        // respect to y brings down two factors of (1+y/f)^(-1), so the second
        // derivative carries v^(i+2).  The i+1 form is the FIRST derivative's
        // exponent and is a textbook-looking, entirely plausible, wrong answer:
        // it overstates convexity by a factor of (1+y/f), which is 3.5% here.
        // See the KAT's finite-difference check, which is what caught it.
        acc = acc + t * (t + inv_f) * cpn * df_pow(v, i + 2);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    let tn: f64 = (n_periods as f64) / f;
    acc = acc + tn * (tn + inv_f) * face * df_pow(v, n_periods + 2);
    return acc / p;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. TERM STRUCTURE — PRICING OFF A ZERO CURVE.
//
// A yield-to-maturity is a single number that reprices one bond.  It is not a
// term structure: two bonds with the same maturity and different coupons have
// different yields even in an arbitrage-free market.  A desk discounts each
// cashflow at ITS OWN maturity's zero rate, which is what this section does.
//
// THE KNOTS ARE EXPLICIT SCALARS, AND WHY.  The curve is passed as four
// (t, z) pairs rather than as an array.  Two reasons:
//   1. mindc 0.10.2 has no f64 load/store intrinsic, so an f64 array must be
//      carried as IEEE bit patterns in an i64 heap block (see
//      `examples/quant/crr_binomial`).  That is correct and proven, but it puts
//      a reinterpret layer between the reader and the curve in the one example
//      whose audience is a risk officer rather than a compiler engineer.
//   2. Four knots — 6M, 2Y, 5Y, 10Y — is a real benchmark curve shape, and
//      making them positional parameters means the KAT below states the entire
//      curve at every call site, with nothing hidden in a constructor.
// A production curve has more knots and belongs in a heap block; this is an
// example, and its job is to be checkable by eye.
//
// INTERPOLATION IS LINEAR IN THE ZERO RATE, deterministically.  The weight is
// formed ONCE as (t - t0) / (t1 - t0) and applied as z0 + w * (z1 - z0), never
// as the algebraically-equal (1-w)*z0 + w*z1.  The two disagree in the last
// bits, and the first form is exact at both endpoints (w = 0 gives exactly z0,
// w = 1 gives exactly z0 + (z1 - z0), which is z1 up to one rounding) while the
// second is not.  Endpoint exactness is what makes the knot-value KAT checks
// below meaningful rather than approximate.
//
// Linear-in-z is a CHOICE, and a contestable one: linear in the log discount
// factor, or monotone cubic on the forwards, are both defensible and produce
// different numbers.  It is stated here rather than assumed, because an
// interpolation convention is exactly the kind of undocumented decision that
// makes two correct systems disagree.
//
// EXTRAPOLATION IS FLAT.  Outside [t0, t3] the nearest knot's rate is used
// unchanged.  Flat extrapolation is the conservative choice — a linear
// extrapolation off the long end can produce negative discount factors at long
// maturities, which is an arbitrage, not a curve.
//
// Discounting is CONTINUOUS: df = exp(-z * t).  That is why `dm_exp` is
// vendored above.  Note the naming discipline: it is `dm_exp`, never `exp`, for
// the libm-capture reason in the header.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn curve_valid(t0: f64, t1: f64, t2: f64, t3: f64) -> i64 {
    if t0 != t0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t1 != t1 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t2 != t2 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t3 != t3 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t0 <= 0.0 {
        return 0;
    }
    // Knots must be strictly increasing, or the interpolation weight divides by
    // zero or by a negative number and the "curve" is not a function of time.
    if t1 <= t0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t2 <= t1 {
        return 0;
    }
    if t3 <= t2 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// Zero rate at maturity t by deterministic linear interpolation between knots,
// flat outside.  Fixed comparison ladder, ascending: the branch taken is a
// function of t alone and nothing else.
pub fn zero_rate(t: f64, t0: f64, z0: f64, t1: f64, z1: f64, t2: f64, z2: f64, t3: f64, z3: f64) -> f64 {
    if t != t {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if curve_valid(t0, t1, t2, t3) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t <= t0 {
        return z0;
    }
    if t >= t3 {
        return z3;
    }
    if t <= t1 {
        let w: f64 = (t - t0) / (t1 - t0);
        return z0 + w * (z1 - z0);
    }
    if t <= t2 {
        let w: f64 = (t - t1) / (t2 - t1);
        return z1 + w * (z2 - z1);
    }
    let w: f64 = (t - t2) / (t3 - t2);
    return z2 + w * (z3 - z2);
}

// Price off the term structure.  Same pinned ascending summation order as
// `bond_price`, same principal-last discipline, same reassociation prohibition.
// The only difference is the discount factor: exp(-z(t) * t) instead of v^i.
pub fn price_from_zero_curve(face: f64, coupon_rate: f64, freq: i64, n_periods: i64, t0: f64, z0: f64, t1: f64, z1: f64, t2: f64, z2: f64, t3: f64, z3: f64) -> f64 {
    if face != face {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if coupon_rate != coupon_rate {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if freq <= 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if n_periods <= 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if face <= 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if coupon_rate < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if curve_valid(t0, t1, t2, t3) == 0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    let f: f64 = freq as f64;
    let cpn: f64 = face * coupon_rate / f;
    let mut acc: f64 = 0.0 * 1.0;
    let mut i: i64 = 1;
    while i <= n_periods {
        let t: f64 = (i as f64) / f;
        let z: f64 = zero_rate(t, t0, z0, t1, z1, t2, z2, t3, z3);
        acc = acc + cpn * dm_exp(0.0 - z * t);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    // Principal repayment, added LAST and as its own rounding step.
    let tn: f64 = (n_periods as f64) / f;
    let zn: f64 = zero_rate(tn, t0, z0, t1, z1, t2, z2, t3, z3);
    acc = acc + face * dm_exp(0.0 - zn * tn);
    return acc;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. IMPLIED FORWARD RATE.
//
//   f(t1, t2) = (z2 * t2 - z1 * t1) / (t2 - t1)
//
// The continuously-compounded forward rate implied by two zeros, by the
// no-arbitrage condition that investing to t2 directly must equal investing to
// t1 and rolling forward:  exp(z2*t2) = exp(z1*t1) * exp(f*(t2-t1)).
//
// The numerator `z2*t2 - z1*t1` is a genuine difference of two same-order
// quantities and it is the cancellation-sensitive term here: for closely-spaced
// maturities with similar rates, the two products agree in their leading digits
// and the difference is formed from what is left.  Dividing that by a small
// (t2 - t1) amplifies whatever error survived.  This is the one function in the
// file whose conditioning is genuinely bad, and it is bad for a structural
// reason rather than an implementation one — no rearrangement fixes it, because
// the quantity itself is a difference over a difference.  A curve with knots
// one day apart will produce a noisy forward in ANY implementation; that is a
// property of the question, not of the arithmetic.
//
// Stated plainly so it is not mistaken for a defect to be fixed later: this
// function is deterministic (the same inputs give the same bits everywhere),
// and it is ill-conditioned (nearby inputs give distant outputs).  Those are
// different properties and this file only claims the first.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
pub fn forward_rate(z1: f64, t1: f64, z2: f64, t2: f64) -> f64 {
    if z1 != z1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t1 != t1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if z2 != z2 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t2 != t2 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    if t1 < 0.0 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    // Strictly increasing, not merely non-equal: t2 == t1 divides by zero, and
    // t2 < t1 silently returns a backwards forward that looks plausible.
    if t2 <= t1 {
        return canonical_qnan();
    }
    return (z2 * t2 - z1 * t1) / (t2 - t1);
}

// ===========================================================================
// KNOWN-ANSWER TESTS.
//
// THE ORACLE.  Every reference value below was produced by an INDEPENDENT
// 50-digit oracle (mpmath, mp.dps = 50) evaluating the closed form in
// near-exact arithmetic and rounded ONCE to f64 — never by this implementation.
// A KAT generated from the code it tests proves only that the code is
// unchanged, not that it is correct.  The oracle script was a throwaway: it is
// not committed, and nothing in this example imports it.
//
// FOUR CLASSES OF CHECK, deliberately, in descending order of value:
//
//   1. STRUCTURAL IDENTITY — relations that must hold regardless of the oracle,
//      and which therefore SURVIVE A BAD REFERENCE.  These are the real
//      deliverable.  A value check tells you the code agrees with one number
//      someone computed; the par-bond identity tells you the discounting is
//      self-consistent, and no amount of wrong reference data can make it pass.
//      The six here are:
//        PAR BOND       — coupon == yield implies price == face, EXACTLY.
//        ROUND TRIP     — ytm(price(y)) == y.  Pricer and solver are mutually
//                         consistent, or one of them is wrong.
//        MONOTONICITY   — price strictly decreasing in yield.
//        CONVEXITY SIGN — strictly positive for a plain vanilla bond.
//        DURATION AS A  — modified duration equals -(1/P) dP/dy, checked
//        SENSITIVITY      against a central finite difference of the pricer.
//        ZERO-COUPON    — a zero's Macaulay duration is EXACTLY its maturity.
//
//   2. VALUE — against the 50-digit oracle, within a stated ulp band.
//
//   3. ADVERSARIAL — a deliberately WRONG reference must be REJECTED.  The
//      acceptance is added straight into `fails`, so a spurious acceptance IS a
//      failure.  Without this block every band above could be passing because
//      it is too wide, and the file would be decorative.
//
//   4. DOMAIN — degenerate inputs return the canonical qNaN, not a plausible
//      number.
//
// ON THE BANDS.  They are TIGHTER here than in the option examples, and that is
// a finding rather than a boast: there is no erfc on this path.  A bond price
// is a sum of products of exactly-rounded divisions and multiplications, so the
// only error is accumulated rounding over n terms, not a re-derived
// transcendental's honest disagreement with a 50-digit reference.  The one
// exception is the curve pricer, which goes through `dm_exp` and therefore
// carries the detmath kernel's error — its bands are correspondingly wider, and
// that difference is visible in the source rather than averaged away by a
// single global tolerance.
//
// WHAT THIS KAT CANNOT CATCH, STATED PLAINLY.
//
//   * REASSOCIATION.  These are tolerance bands, so they are structurally blind
//     to any change that perturbs the result by less than the band — including
//     the summation-order changes that the comments above spend the most words
//     forbidding.  Summing the coupon leg descending instead of ascending would
//     pass every check in this file.  Detecting that requires a BIT-EXACT
//     reference, which is the job of the cross-substrate CI fixture
//     (tests/cross_substrate_identity.rs): it compares hashes across x86 `avx2`
//     and ARM `neon`, and a hash has no tolerance to hide in.  This KAT proves
//     the analytics are CORRECT; the fixture proves they are REPRODUCIBLE.  A
//     green KAT is not evidence of byte-identity, and must never be cited as
//     such.
//
//   * CONVENTION ERRORS.  Everything here is whole-period and clean-price.  If
//     the surrounding system computes accrued interest wrongly, or uses 30/360
//     where the prospectus says ACT/ACT, every check in this file still passes
//     and the booked number is still wrong.  This file gates the arithmetic,
//     not the contract.
//
//   * THE SOLVER'S PATH.  The round-trip identity proves `bond_ytm` finds the
//     right root.  It does not prove the iteration took the same PATH it took
//     yesterday — a change from 80 to 90 iterations, or a different Newton
//     guard, would still converge to a yield inside the round-trip band.  Only
//     the cross-substrate hash pins the path.
// ===========================================================================

fn chk_ulp(got: f64, want: f64, ulps: f64) -> i64 {
    let eps: f64 = 0.0000000000000002220446049250313;
    let tol: f64 = ulps * eps * absf(want);
    if absf(got - want) <= tol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn chk_abs(got: f64, want: f64, abstol: f64) -> i64 {
    if absf(got - want) <= abstol {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn is_nan(x: f64) -> i64 {
    if x != x {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Strict inequality as a counted check: 1 when `a > b`, else 0.  Written as a
// helper so the monotonicity block below reads as a sequence of claims rather
// than a wall of if/else, and so a mis-typed comparison is in ONE place.
fn chk_gt(a: f64, b: f64) -> i64 {
    if a > b {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// The accumulation bound for an n-term sum of quantities of magnitude `scale`.
//
// DERIVED, NOT TUNED, and this distinction is the whole point of the function
// existing.  Summing n terms left to right accumulates at most n roundings,
// each of at most eps/2 relative to the running total, so the absolute error is
// bounded by about n * eps * scale.  The factor 4 is engineering margin for the
// per-term rounding in `df_pow` (which is itself a chain of multiplies) on top
// of the summation itself.
//
// This matters because the par-bond identity below is EXACT in real arithmetic
// and only approximate in f64.  The temptation is to write a round number like
// 1e-12 and move on.  That was in fact the first draft, and it FAILED: the
// measured residue for the 10-year semiannual par bond is 1.56e-13, which is
// comfortable against 1e-12 until you try the 15-year quarterly bond, where the
// residue is 3.18e-12 and a flat 1e-12 band rejects a CORRECT price.  A band
// that has to be widened whenever a new case is added is not a band, it is a
// record of which cases have been tried.  Deriving it from n and the scale
// makes it predict rather than follow.
fn accum_band(n_periods: i64, scale: f64) -> f64 {
    let eps: f64 = 0.0000000000000002220446049250313;
    return 4.0 * (n_periods as f64) * eps * scale;
}

pub fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut fails: i64 = 0;

    // The benchmark curve used by every curve check below: 6M at 3.00%, 2Y at
    // 3.50%, 5Y at 4.00%, 10Y at 4.30%.  An ordinary upward-sloping shape.
    let ct0: f64 = 0.5;
    let cz0: f64 = 0.03;
    let ct1: f64 = 2.0;
    let cz1: f64 = 0.035;
    let ct2: f64 = 5.0;
    let cz2: f64 = 0.04;
    let ct3: f64 = 10.0;
    let cz3: f64 = 0.043;

    // =====================================================================
    // 1. STRUCTURAL IDENTITIES.  These survive a bad oracle.
    // =====================================================================

    // --- PAR BOND: coupon == yield implies price == face, EXACTLY ----------
    // The strongest self-checking identity available for a bond.  It is a
    // theorem, not a reference value: the coupon leg is an annuity worth
    // face*(1 - v^n) and the principal is worth face*v^n, and the two sum to
    // face for any n and any f whenever c == y.  A sign error, a missing
    // principal repayment, an off-by-one in the period index, or a discount
    // factor raised to the wrong power all break it, and NONE of them requires
    // a correct reference number to detect.
    //
    // The band is the DERIVED accumulation bound `accum_band(n, face)`, not a
    // round number — see that function's comment for why the round number was
    // wrong.  The identity is exact in real arithmetic; the residue is the n
    // roundings of the f64 sum, and asserting exact equality would be asserting
    // something false about floating point rather than something true about
    // the bond.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20), 100.0, accum_band(20, 100.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_price(100.0, 0.07, 0.07, 2, 20), 100.0, accum_band(20, 100.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_price(1000.0, 0.03, 0.03, 4, 60), 1000.0, accum_band(60, 1000.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_price(100.0, 0.06, 0.06, 1, 7), 100.0, accum_band(7, 100.0)));
    // Par at a very low yield, where `1 + y/f` is close to 1 and the repeated
    // multiply in `df_pow` has the least per-step rounding to work with.
    //
    // NOTE ON THE CASE THAT IS NOT HERE, because its absence is a design
    // decision rather than an oversight.  The natural next case is par at a
    // NEGATIVE yield — a -1% two-year Bund is an ordinary instrument and the
    // pricer must handle it, which it does (see the monotonicity chain below,
    // whose first point is y = -1%).  But the PAR identity requires c == y, and
    // `bond_valid` rejects a negative COUPON: a bond that charges its holder a
    // coupon is not an instrument, it is a malformed request, and the guard
    // says so.  So par-at-negative-yield is unreachable by construction, and
    // the negative-rate claim is carried by the monotonicity chain instead.
    // The first draft of this file asserted it anyway; it failed, and the
    // failure was the guard working correctly.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_price(100.0, 0.001, 0.001, 2, 10), 100.0, accum_band(10, 100.0)));

    // --- ROUND TRIP: bond_ytm(bond_price(y)) == y --------------------------
    // Proves the pricer and the solver are mutually consistent.  Neither can be
    // wrong alone and still pass: the solver inverts the pricer, so a shared
    // error would have to be an error that is its own inverse.
    //
    // Band 1e-12 absolute on a yield, i.e. a hundredth of a basis point of a
    // basis point.  Deliberately far tighter than any economically meaningful
    // tolerance — this is a numerical identity, not a market quote, and a loose
    // band here would let a genuinely broken solver through.
    let rt_a: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_ytm(rt_a, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 20), 0.07, 0.000000000001));
    let rt_b: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 2, 20);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_ytm(rt_b, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 20), 0.03, 0.000000000001));
    let rt_c: f64 = bond_price(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425, 2, 30);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_ytm(rt_c, 1000.0, 0.065, 2, 30), 0.0425, 0.000000000001));
    let rt_d: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.08, 0.06, 4, 40);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_ytm(rt_d, 100.0, 0.08, 4, 40), 0.06, 0.000000000001));
    // At par: the round trip must return the coupon exactly, which is the
    // intersection of the two identities above.
    let rt_e: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_ytm(rt_e, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 20), 0.05, 0.000000000001));
    // A zero-coupon bond, where the yield has a closed form and the Newton
    // derivative is a single term.  Included because a solver that relies on
    // the coupon leg to shape the objective degenerates here.
    let rt_f: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.0, 0.04, 1, 10);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_ytm(rt_f, 100.0, 0.0, 1, 10), 0.04, 0.000000000001));
    // A deeply discounted long bond: yield well above coupon, 30 years.  The
    // bracket must survive a price far from par.
    let rt_g: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.02, 0.09, 2, 60);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(bond_ytm(rt_g, 100.0, 0.02, 2, 60), 0.09, 0.000000000001));

    // --- PRICE / YIELD MONOTONICITY: strictly DECREASING in yield ----------
    // The single most basic property of a bond, and the one a sign error
    // destroys first.  Asserted as a chain of strict inequalities across the
    // whole admissible range including negative yields, not just at two points:
    // a chain catches a non-monotone kink that a single pair would step over.
    let m1: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.0 - 0.01, 2, 20);
    let m2: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.01, 2, 20);
    let m3: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 2, 20);
    let m4: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20);
    let m5: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20);
    let m6: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.12, 2, 20);
    let m7: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.25, 2, 20);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(m1, m2));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(m2, m3));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(m3, m4));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(m4, m5));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(m5, m6));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(m6, m7));
    // The same chain for a zero-coupon bond, where there is no coupon leg to
    // mask a broken principal discount factor.
    let z1p: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.0, 0.01, 1, 10);
    let z2p: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.0, 0.04, 1, 10);
    let z3p: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.0, 0.09, 1, 10);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(z1p, z2p));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(z2p, z3p));

    // --- CONVEXITY SIGN: strictly positive for a plain vanilla bond --------
    // A positive-cashflow instrument has a price that is a positive-weighted
    // sum of convex discount functions, so its convexity is strictly positive
    // everywhere.  Negative convexity is a real phenomenon — callable bonds and
    // mortgage-backed securities have it — but it cannot arise here, so a
    // non-positive value is a bug and not a market feature.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(convexity(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20), 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(convexity(100.0, 0.05, 0.12, 2, 20), 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(convexity(100.0, 0.0, 0.04, 1, 10), 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(convexity(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425, 2, 30), 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(convexity(100.0, 0.05, 0.0 - 0.01, 2, 20), 0.0));
    // Duration is strictly positive too, and strictly less than maturity for
    // any coupon-bearing bond (the coupons pull the average cashflow time in).
    // That inequality is a second structural claim on the same number.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20), 0.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(10.0, macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20)));

    // --- DURATION AS A SENSITIVITY: modified D vs a central difference ------
    // Modified duration is DEFINED as -(1/P) dP/dy.  The analytic sum above is
    // one way to compute it; a central finite difference of the pricer is a
    // genuinely independent one, sharing only the pricer itself.  Agreement
    // between them falsifies the whole family of algebra errors in the duration
    // formula — a missing 1/f, a t_i off by one period, the modified/Macaulay
    // division applied to the wrong quantity.
    //
    // THIS IS THE ONLY FINITE DIFFERENCE IN THE FILE, and it is a CHECK, never
    // an implementation.  See the section-3 comment.
    //
    // THE BAND IS DERIVED, NOT TUNED.  A central difference has truncation
    // error (h^2/6) * P'''/P and rounding error about (2*eps*P)/(2*h*P).  With
    // h = 1e-5 and a third derivative of order D^3 ~ 500 for these bonds:
    //   truncation ~ 1e-10/6 * 500      ~ 8e-9
    //   rounding    ~ 2.2e-16 / 1e-5    ~ 2.2e-11
    // so about 1e-8 in total, against a duration of order 8.  The band below is
    // 1e-6 absolute — two orders of magnitude of headroom over the derived
    // bound, because the point of this check is to catch a structurally wrong
    // formula (which is wrong by 1/f, or by a factor of 1+y/f, i.e. by
    // PERCENT), not to measure the finite difference.  A band tight enough to
    // resolve the difference scheme would be a check on the difference scheme.
    let h: f64 = 0.00001;
    let fd_p_up: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.07 + h, 2, 20);
    let fd_p_dn: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.07 - h, 2, 20);
    let fd_p_0: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20);
    let fd_md: f64 = 0.0 - (fd_p_up - fd_p_dn) / (2.0 * h) / fd_p_0;
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(modified_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), fd_md, 0.000001));

    // Same check at a second point and a different frequency, so a coincidence
    // at one yield cannot carry the claim.
    let g_up: f64 = bond_price(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425 + h, 2, 30);
    let g_dn: f64 = bond_price(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425 - h, 2, 30);
    let g_0: f64 = bond_price(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425, 2, 30);
    let g_md: f64 = 0.0 - (g_up - g_dn) / (2.0 * h) / g_0;
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(modified_duration(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425, 2, 30), g_md, 0.000001));

    let q_up: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.08, 0.06 + h, 4, 40);
    let q_dn: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.08, 0.06 - h, 4, 40);
    let q_0: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.08, 0.06, 4, 40);
    let q_md: f64 = 0.0 - (q_up - q_dn) / (2.0 * h) / q_0;
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(modified_duration(100.0, 0.08, 0.06, 4, 40), q_md, 0.000001));

    // Convexity against a SECOND central difference of the pricer.
    //
    // THIS CHECK FOUND A REAL BUG, and the bug is worth recording because it is
    // exactly the kind a value check cannot find.  The first draft of
    // `convexity` used the discount exponent v^(i+1).  That is the FIRST
    // derivative's exponent; differentiating v^i twice brings down two factors
    // of (1+y/f)^(-1), so the second derivative carries v^(i+2).  The i+1 form
    // overstates convexity by exactly (1+y/f) — 3.5% here — and it looks
    // completely plausible.  It also matched the 50-digit oracle, because the
    // oracle script was derived from the same wrong algebra.  A value check
    // whose reference shares the implementation's error is not a check at all;
    // this finite difference, which shares only the PRICER, is what caught it.
    // That is the argument for structural checks in one concrete instance.
    //
    // THE STEP SIZE IS ITS OWN, AND LARGER THAN THE FIRST-DIFFERENCE ONE.  A
    // second difference divides a threefold cancellation by h^2, so its
    // rounding error scales as eps/h^2 rather than eps/h: at h = 1e-5 that is
    // 2.2e-6 relative on a quantity of order 70, i.e. about 3e-5 absolute, and
    // at h = 1e-3 the truncation term (h^2/12)*P''''/P has grown to 6e-4.  The
    // minimum sits near h = 1e-4, where the measured disagreement with the
    // analytic form is 6.1e-6.  The band below is 1e-3 absolute — about 160x
    // that measured error, and still 2400x SMALLER than the 2.45 absolute
    // discrepancy the v^(i+1) bug produced.  Headroom over the noise, decisive
    // against the bug: that is what a useful band looks like.
    let h2: f64 = 0.0001;
    let cv_up: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.07 + h2, 2, 20);
    let cv_dn: f64 = bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.07 - h2, 2, 20);
    let cv_2nd: f64 = (cv_up - 2.0 * fd_p_0 + cv_dn) / (h2 * h2) / fd_p_0;
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(convexity(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), cv_2nd, 0.001));
    // The adversarial twin, inline: the v^(i+1) bug scaled convexity by
    // (1 + y/f) = 1.035.  Feeding that scaling to the same band must be
    // REJECTED, which proves this check would have caught the bug it did in
    // fact catch — and will catch it again if someone "simplifies" the exponent
    // back.  A regression test for a fixed bug is only worth having if it is
    // known to fail on the bug.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(convexity(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20) * 1.035, cv_2nd, 0.001);

    // --- ZERO-COUPON: Macaulay duration EXACTLY equals maturity ------------
    // With one cashflow at t = n/f, the weighted average time is that cashflow's
    // time, and the weight is 1 by construction.  The division acc/P is then a
    // quantity divided by itself times t, so the result is t to within one
    // rounding — this is the tightest identity in the file and the band says so.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.0, 0.04, 1, 10), 10.0, 0.0000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.0, 0.07, 2, 20), 10.0, 0.0000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(macaulay_duration(1000.0, 0.0, 0.02, 4, 30), 7.5, 0.0000000000001));
    // And its modified duration is exactly maturity / (1 + y/f).
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(modified_duration(100.0, 0.0, 0.04, 1, 10), 10.0 / 1.04, 0.0000000000001));

    // =====================================================================
    // 2. VALUE CHECKS against the 50-digit oracle (mpmath, mp.dps = 50).
    // =====================================================================

    // --- Case A: face=100 c=5% y=5% semiannual 10y (AT PAR) ----------------
    // Oracle price is exactly 100 (see the par identity above); duration and
    // convexity are not, and are checked here as values.
    // Oracle macaulay  = 7.989445671393989756961995...
    // Oracle modified  = 7.794581142823404630385210...
    // Oracle convexity = 73.62873142656359731544489...  (v^(i+2) form; see the
    // finite-difference check above, which is what established the exponent)
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20), 7.98944567139399, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(modified_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20), 7.794581142823405, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(convexity(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20), 73.6287314265636, 256.0));

    // --- Case B: face=100 c=5% y=7% semiannual 10y (DISCOUNT) --------------
    // Oracle price = 85.78759669804770709874087...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), 85.78759669804771, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), 7.797649249801262, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(modified_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), 7.533960627827306, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(convexity(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), 69.94360735422342, 256.0));

    // --- Case C: face=100 c=5% y=3% semiannual 10y (PREMIUM) ---------------
    // Oracle price = 117.1686387850819335867448...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 2, 20), 117.16863878508194, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 2, 20), 8.169425098281543, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(modified_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 2, 20), 8.048694678109895, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(convexity(100.0, 0.05, 0.03, 2, 20), 77.31559697849131, 256.0));

    // --- Case D: face=1000 c=6.5% y=4.25% semiannual 15y -------------------
    // A long premium bond at a non-round yield: 30 periods, so the accumulated
    // rounding in the ascending sum is at its largest in this file.
    // Oracle price = 1247.682798367062294291032...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bond_price(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425, 2, 30), 1247.6827983670623, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(macaulay_duration(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425, 2, 30), 10.393494633137566, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(modified_duration(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425, 2, 30), 10.17722852694009, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(convexity(1000.0, 0.065, 0.0425, 2, 30), 133.78712621661745, 256.0));

    // --- Case E: face=100 ZERO COUPON y=4% annual 10y ----------------------
    // Oracle price = 67.55641688257988511113267...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bond_price(100.0, 0.0, 0.04, 1, 10), 67.55641688257988, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(modified_duration(100.0, 0.0, 0.04, 1, 10), 9.615384615384615, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(convexity(100.0, 0.0, 0.04, 1, 10), 101.70118343195266, 256.0));

    // --- Case F: face=100 c=8% y=6% QUARTERLY 10y --------------------------
    // The only f = 4 value case: 40 periods, and the one that catches a 1/f
    // dropped from a time index (which is invisible at f = 1 and off by a
    // factor of 2 at f = 2).
    // Oracle price = 114.9579226020872867040604...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bond_price(100.0, 0.08, 0.06, 4, 40), 114.9579226020873, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.08, 0.06, 4, 40), 7.206103213052824, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(modified_duration(100.0, 0.08, 0.06, 4, 40), 7.099609076899334, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(convexity(100.0, 0.08, 0.06, 4, 40), 63.26780838641731, 256.0));

    // --- Case G: face=100 c=3% y=5.5% ANNUAL 5y ----------------------------
    // Short and annual: the smallest n in the file, where a per-period error
    // has the least room to average out.
    // Oracle price = 89.32428881095223697618195...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(bond_price(100.0, 0.03, 0.055, 1, 5), 89.32428881095224, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.03, 0.055, 1, 5), 4.697822262022155, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(modified_duration(100.0, 0.03, 0.055, 1, 5), 4.452912096703464, 256.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(convexity(100.0, 0.03, 0.055, 1, 5), 24.795931073172472, 256.0));

    // --- ZERO CURVE: interpolation at and between the knots ----------------
    // AT the knots the interpolation must return the knot rate EXACTLY, which
    // is why the weight is applied as z0 + w*(z1 - z0) rather than the
    // algebraically-equal (1-w)*z0 + w*z1 — see the section-4 comment.  These
    // are exact-equality-band checks (1e-18), not tolerance checks.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(zero_rate(0.5, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.03, 0.000000000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(zero_rate(2.0, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.035, 0.000000000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(zero_rate(5.0, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.04, 0.000000000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(zero_rate(10.0, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.043, 0.000000000000000001));
    // BETWEEN the knots, against the 50-digit oracle.
    // Oracle zr(1.0)  = 0.03166666666666666666666667...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(zero_rate(1.0, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.03166666666666667, 64.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(zero_rate(1.25, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.0325, 64.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(zero_rate(3.5, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.0375, 64.0));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(zero_rate(7.0, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.0412, 64.0));
    // OUTSIDE the knots, flat extrapolation both ends.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(zero_rate(0.25, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.03, 0.000000000000000001));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(zero_rate(12.0, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 0.043, 0.000000000000000001));
    // Monotone curve, monotone interpolation: the rate is non-decreasing in t
    // on this upward-sloping curve.  A structural claim on the interpolator
    // that no reference value can supply.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(zero_rate(3.5, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), zero_rate(1.0, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3)));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(zero_rate(7.0, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), zero_rate(3.5, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3)));

    // --- CURVE PRICING -----------------------------------------------------
    // Wider bands than the yield-based prices: this path goes through `dm_exp`,
    // so it carries the detmath kernel's honest disagreement with the 50-digit
    // oracle on top of the summation rounding.  The difference in band width
    // between these checks and the ones above is exactly the cost of the
    // transcendental, made visible rather than averaged away.
    // Oracle H = 104.4723057871062524554271...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(price_from_zero_curve(100.0, 0.05, 2, 10, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 104.47230578710625, 4096.0));
    // Oracle I = 97.27466692859226398403442...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(price_from_zero_curve(100.0, 0.04, 1, 10, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 97.27466692859227, 4096.0));
    // Oracle J = 89.58341352965282506768546...  (3y zero coupon off the curve)
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(price_from_zero_curve(100.0, 0.0, 1, 3, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 89.58341352965283, 4096.0));

    // --- CURVE IDENTITY: a FLAT curve must reprice as continuous discounting -
    // With all four knots at the same rate z, every cashflow discounts at
    // exp(-z*t) regardless of interpolation, so the curve price must equal the
    // hand-built sum.  This is a structural check on the interpolation and the
    // cashflow schedule TOGETHER, and it holds whatever the oracle says.
    let flat_z: f64 = 0.04;
    let flat_curve: f64 = price_from_zero_curve(100.0, 0.05, 1, 5, 0.5, flat_z, 2.0, flat_z, 5.0, flat_z, 10.0, flat_z);
    // The same five cashflows, discounted by hand in the same ascending order.
    let mut flat_hand: f64 = 0.0 * 1.0;
    let mut fi: i64 = 1;
    while fi <= 5 {
        let ft: f64 = (fi as f64) / 1.0;
        flat_hand = flat_hand + 5.0 * dm_exp(0.0 - flat_z * ft);
        fi = fi + 1;
    }
    flat_hand = flat_hand + 100.0 * dm_exp(0.0 - flat_z * 5.0);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(flat_curve, flat_hand, 0.0000000001));

    // --- CURVE IDENTITY: a higher curve prices lower ------------------------
    // Monotonicity in the level of the curve, the term-structure analogue of
    // the price/yield monotonicity above.
    let lvl_lo: f64 = price_from_zero_curve(100.0, 0.05, 2, 10, 0.5, 0.02, 2.0, 0.025, 5.0, 0.03, 10.0, 0.033);
    let lvl_hi: f64 = price_from_zero_curve(100.0, 0.05, 2, 10, 0.5, 0.05, 2.0, 0.055, 5.0, 0.06, 10.0, 0.063);
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(lvl_lo, lvl_hi));

    // --- FORWARD RATES ------------------------------------------------------
    // Oracle f(1,2) from z=3% and z=4% is exactly 5%: (0.04*2 - 0.03*1)/1.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(forward_rate(0.03, 1.0, 0.04, 2.0), 0.05, 256.0));
    // Oracle f(2,5)  = 0.04333333333333333250066606...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(forward_rate(0.035, 2.0, 0.04, 5.0), 0.043333333333333335, 256.0));
    // Oracle f(5,10) = 0.04599999999999999228394998...
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_ulp(forward_rate(0.04, 5.0, 0.043, 10.0), 0.04599999999999999, 256.0));
    // STRUCTURAL: on an upward-sloping curve the forward is above the far zero,
    // which is the no-arbitrage statement that forwards lead spots.  This holds
    // by construction for any increasing z(t) and needs no reference value.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(forward_rate(0.035, 2.0, 0.04, 5.0), 0.04));
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_gt(forward_rate(0.04, 5.0, 0.043, 10.0), 0.043));
    // STRUCTURAL: on a FLAT curve the forward equals the zero, exactly.  A
    // degenerate case that catches a transposed t1/t2 in the numerator, which
    // the sloping cases above would only show as a plausible wrong number.
    fails = fails + (1 - chk_abs(forward_rate(0.04, 1.0, 0.04, 5.0), 0.04, 0.0000000000001));

    // =====================================================================
    // 3. ADVERSARIAL: the bands MUST reject wrong values.
    //
    // Each line adds the ACCEPTANCE to `fails`, so a spurious acceptance is a
    // failure.  Without this block every check above could be passing because
    // its band is too wide, and a green exit code would mean nothing.  The
    // wrong values are deliberately close to the right ones — a few parts in
    // 1e4, not orders of magnitude — because a band that only rejects absurd
    // values is not a band.
    // =====================================================================

    // Price, wrong in the third decimal.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), 85.78859669804771, 256.0);
    // Duration, wrong in the fourth decimal.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), 7.798649249801262, 256.0);
    // Modified duration confused with Macaulay: the two differ by the factor
    // (1 + y/f), i.e. by 3.5% here.  This is THE most common implementation
    // error in a duration routine and it must not pass.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(modified_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), 7.797649249801262, 256.0);
    // Convexity, wrong by one part in 1e4.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(convexity(100.0, 0.05, 0.07, 2, 20), 69.95360735422342, 256.0);
    // A par bond that is NOT 100: the par identity's derived band must reject a
    // price one part in 1e10 away.  `accum_band(20, 100.0)` is 1.78e-12, so the
    // 1e-8 offset is about 5600x outside it — the check proves the DERIVED band
    // discriminates, which matters more than the flat one did, because a
    // derived band is the kind that silently grows if the derivation is wrong.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20), 100.00000001, accum_band(20, 100.0));
    // A round trip that returns the wrong yield by a tenth of a basis point.
    // The round-trip band is 1e-12; 1e-4 is eight orders of magnitude outside
    // it, and a solver that returned this would look entirely plausible on a
    // trading screen.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(bond_ytm(rt_a, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 20), 0.0701, 0.000000000001);
    // A zero-coupon Macaulay duration that is not the maturity.  This is the
    // adversarial form of the tightest identity in the file.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.0, 0.04, 1, 10), 10.0000001, 0.0000000000001);
    // A curve price wrong in the fourth decimal, against the WIDER 4096-ulp
    // band — proving that even the loosest band in the file discriminates.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(price_from_zero_curve(100.0, 0.05, 2, 10, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3), 104.47330578710626, 4096.0);
    // A forward rate computed with t1 and t2 transposed in the numerator:
    // (z2*t1 - z1*t2)/(t2-t1) instead of (z2*t2 - z1*t1)/(t2-t1).  For the
    // (2y, 5y) pair the wrong form gives -0.03166..., so this one is far from
    // the right answer — which is the point: the check proves the band cannot
    // be argued into accepting a sign-scale error.
    fails = fails + chk_ulp(forward_rate(0.035, 2.0, 0.04, 5.0), 0.0 - 0.03166666666666667, 256.0);
    // The finite-difference duration check, fed a duration wrong by 1/f.  A
    // dropped 1/f is the second most common duration bug and it is a factor-of-2
    // error at semiannual frequency; the 1e-6 band must reject it decisively.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(fd_md * 2.0, fd_md, 0.000001);
    // The flat-curve identity, fed a hand sum missing its principal repayment.
    // This is the adversarial twin of the mutation test run against this file:
    // dropping the principal is the single most damaging silent error in a
    // cashflow pricer, and the identity must not survive it.
    fails = fails + chk_abs(flat_curve, flat_hand - 100.0 * dm_exp(0.0 - flat_z * 5.0), 0.0000000001);

    // =====================================================================
    // 4. DOMAIN: degenerate inputs must return the canonical qNaN.
    //
    // Not a plausible-looking number.  Every one of these is a caller error,
    // and the function says so instead of guessing.  A bond priced with zero
    // periods is not worth its face value; it is a malformed request.
    // =====================================================================

    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 0, 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 0 - 2, 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 0 - 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_price(0.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_price(0.0 - 100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_price(100.0, 0.0 - 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20)));
    // Yield at or below -f: the single-period discount factor is infinite or
    // negative and the "price" would be arithmetic noise.  At f = 2 the bound
    // is y = -2.0.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.0 - 2.0, 2, 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_price(100.0, 0.05, 0.0 - 3.0, 2, 20)));

    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(macaulay_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 0, 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(modified_duration(100.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(convexity(0.0, 0.05, 0.05, 2, 20)));

    // The solver's domain: a price no yield in the bracket can produce must be
    // REFUSED, not converged to an endpoint.
    //
    // THE BRACKET IS WIDER THAN IT LOOKS, and a draft of this block asserted
    // otherwise.  For the 10-year 5% semiannual bond the admissible price band
    // is [2.50, 16452320.43]: the upper end is the price at YTM_LO = -90%,
    // where the discount factor is 1/(1 - 0.45) per period compounded twenty
    // times.  A price of 1,000,000 is therefore INSIDE the bracket and the
    // solver correctly returns a yield for it — absurd as an instrument, but a
    // legitimate inverse.  Asserting qNaN there was asserting a fact about the
    // model that is not true, and the check failed until it was measured rather
    // than assumed.  The rejection cases below are outside the MEASURED band.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_ytm(0.0, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_ytm(0.0 - 50.0, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 20)));
    // Above the price at YTM_LO = -90% (16,452,320.43), so unreachable.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_ytm(100000000.0, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_ytm(85.0, 100.0, 0.05, 0, 20)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_ytm(85.0, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 0)));
    // Below the price at YTM_HI = 200% (2.50), so likewise unreachable.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(bond_ytm(0.0000001, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 20)));
    // And the POSITIVE CONTROL for this whole block: a price that IS in the
    // bracket must NOT be refused.  Without it, a `bond_ytm` that returned qNaN
    // unconditionally would pass every rejection check above — the classic
    // vacuous negative assertion.
    fails = fails + is_nan(bond_ytm(85.78759669804771, 100.0, 0.05, 2, 20));

    // Curve domain: non-increasing knots are not a curve.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(zero_rate(1.0, 2.0, 0.03, 2.0, 0.035, 5.0, 0.04, 10.0, 0.043)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(zero_rate(1.0, 0.5, 0.03, 0.25, 0.035, 5.0, 0.04, 10.0, 0.043)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(zero_rate(1.0, 0.0 - 1.0, 0.03, 2.0, 0.035, 5.0, 0.04, 10.0, 0.043)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(price_from_zero_curve(100.0, 0.05, 0, 10, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(price_from_zero_curve(100.0, 0.05, 2, 0, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(price_from_zero_curve(0.0, 0.05, 2, 10, ct0, cz0, ct1, cz1, ct2, cz2, ct3, cz3)));

    // Forward-rate domain: equal or inverted maturities.
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(forward_rate(0.03, 2.0, 0.04, 2.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(forward_rate(0.03, 5.0, 0.04, 2.0)));
    fails = fails + (1 - is_nan(forward_rate(0.03, 0.0 - 1.0, 0.04, 2.0)));

    return fails;
}
